WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.312 --> 00:00:06.711
Well, welcome everybody,
great to see you.

00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:09.070
We are lucky enough today
to have David Mohr from

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:13.160
Northwestern University here
joining us today to talk about

00:00:13.161 --> 00:00:17.020
his great work that he's been
doing on the crossroads between

00:00:17.021 --> 00:00:21.790
behaviour change, technology,
and serious mental health.

00:00:21.791 --> 00:00:25.478
David is one of the pioneers
using mobile technology,

00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:28.934
sensing platforms, and
suites of applications.

00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:32.339
And really studying their
efficacy with real users who

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.271
have real problems over time.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.180
Weaves this technology
into their everyday lives,

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:40.210
which is what I really
appreciate about it.

00:00:40.211 --> 00:00:43.290
And I'm gonna stop there and
let him tell you about it.

00:00:43.291 --> 00:00:45.980
I forgot to say that he's also
in the School of Medicine at

00:00:45.981 --> 00:00:50.270
Northwestern and he's the
director of CBIT, which stands

00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:53.160
for the Center for Behavioral
Intervention Technologies which

00:00:53.161 --> 00:00:54.230
is very well known.

00:00:54.231 --> 00:00:56.085
So welcome, David.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:02.938
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thanks very, very much.

00:01:02.939 --> 00:01:06.096
It's really a pleasure to be
here to spend some time with

00:01:06.097 --> 00:01:08.280
Mary and
to get to know some of you.

00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:14.297
So thank you for
inviting me out.

00:01:14.298 --> 00:01:19.680
First, I'm a clinical
psychologist by training.

00:01:19.681 --> 00:01:23.700
And I've collaborated with
people in HCI and computer

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:26.860
science and engineering enough
to get myself into trouble.

00:01:26.861 --> 00:01:30.090
But that's not the area that
I know that much about.

00:01:30.091 --> 00:01:30.870
So most of

00:01:30.871 --> 00:01:33.660
this is gonna be from the
perspective of a psychologist.

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:34.990
So I'll start off
with just a few

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.698
slides to kind of set the frame
work in terms of psychology.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:41.110
The I'm gonna talk
a little bit about

00:01:41.111 --> 00:01:43.940
kind of a new direction that we
have, Mary already eluded to

00:01:43.941 --> 00:01:46.170
that we call Intelicare,
a platform of applications and

00:01:46.171 --> 00:01:47.470
then talk a little
bit about sensing.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.440
So, I have to mention this,
there's disclosures.

00:01:54.441 --> 00:01:56.700
So I've had consulting fees
from Otsuka and Optum.

00:01:56.701 --> 00:01:59.160
And then I have an ownership
interest in Actualize Health,

00:01:59.161 --> 00:02:01.820
which is trying to commercialize
some of the things we're

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:02.390
talking about.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.990
So, mental health, most of
what I'm gonna focus on is

00:02:07.991 --> 00:02:10.400
common mental health problems
like depressions and anxiety.

00:02:10.401 --> 00:02:12.552
But mental health
problems are common.

00:02:12.553 --> 00:02:17.085
So around 20 to 25% of Americans
will have a mental health

00:02:17.086 --> 00:02:19.280
problem in any given year.

00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:22.180
And the common ones
like mood disorders and

00:02:22.181 --> 00:02:25.680
anxiety disorders you can see
that the prevalence rates there.

00:02:25.681 --> 00:02:28.052
Our workforce is inadequate.

00:02:28.053 --> 00:02:30.811
So only around 20% of
Americans receive what's

00:02:30.812 --> 00:02:32.680
called minimally adequate care.

00:02:32.681 --> 00:02:36.265
And believe me when you look
at what minimally adequate is,

00:02:36.266 --> 00:02:38.570
it is far more minimal
than adequate.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.870
And if we have about
half the number of mental

00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:46.950
health professionals that we
would need to treat people,

00:02:46.951 --> 00:02:49.340
and we're never gonna
have enough people.

00:02:49.341 --> 00:02:51.680
And even if we had them,

00:02:51.681 --> 00:02:54.080
they wouldn't necessarily
be where the patient are.

00:02:54.081 --> 00:02:56.820
So 90 million Americans live in

00:02:56.821 --> 00:02:59.500
federal designated mental
profession shortage areas,

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:04.025
higher than any other
medical specialty.

00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:08.355
So this is the argument for
digital mental health.

00:03:08.356 --> 00:03:10.635
When we think about treating
people with mental health

00:03:10.636 --> 00:03:13.855
problems, behaviorally or
with psychological treatments,

00:03:13.856 --> 00:03:17.265
usually there's some kind of,
we've tended to use

00:03:18.515 --> 00:03:21.705
theories that were developed for
psychotherapy.

00:03:21.706 --> 00:03:23.905
And this is one of
the most common ones,

00:03:23.906 --> 00:03:25.750
there are a lot of them.

00:03:25.751 --> 00:03:29.513
And so this theory,
cognitive behavioral theory,

00:03:29.514 --> 00:03:33.703
starts from the premise that
when people are experiencing

00:03:33.704 --> 00:03:37.894
emotional disorders,
we can't reach inside, like with

00:03:37.895 --> 00:03:42.191
a pharmaceutical, reach inside
and change that feeling.

00:03:42.192 --> 00:03:44.945
But we can leverage the fact
that our thoughts, and

00:03:44.946 --> 00:03:48.220
our behaviors, and our feelings
are all affected, right?

00:03:48.221 --> 00:03:51.950
So if I came in here and I were
thinking these people, man,

00:03:51.951 --> 00:03:52.670
they're really smart.

00:03:52.671 --> 00:03:54.520
They're gonna judge
me really harshly.

00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:55.620
I better do a good job.

00:03:55.621 --> 00:03:57.631
I would be nervous.

00:03:57.632 --> 00:03:59.452
It would affect my thoughts.

00:03:59.453 --> 00:04:01.030
It would make me feel nervous.

00:04:01.031 --> 00:04:03.690
I might be jittery in
my behaviors, right?

00:04:03.691 --> 00:04:06.525
But if I come in and think, well
you know they might have some

00:04:06.526 --> 00:04:08.065
disagreements with what I say.

00:04:08.066 --> 00:04:10.360
And we could get into some
interesting discussions.

00:04:10.361 --> 00:04:11.710
My whole approach, my thoughts,

00:04:11.711 --> 00:04:14.660
my feelings will be different,
right?

00:04:14.661 --> 00:04:16.740
So we usually start
with a theory.

00:04:16.741 --> 00:04:18.190
I'm not sure if
that's a good idea,

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:19.990
that's what we've done
up until now, but

00:04:19.991 --> 00:04:21.350
I just wanna put that out there.

00:04:21.351 --> 00:04:23.960
The other thing I wanna
talk about is some features

00:04:23.961 --> 00:04:28.890
of treatment for mental health
problems that we should keep

00:04:28.891 --> 00:04:30.890
in mind when we're thinking
about treating mental health.

00:04:30.891 --> 00:04:33.113
So one is, they're slow.

00:04:33.114 --> 00:04:35.868
It's not like you take
a pill and you get better.

00:04:35.869 --> 00:04:39.000
Psychotherapy or pharmacotherapy
takes at least four

00:04:39.001 --> 00:04:41.540
weeks even to have
the first impact.

00:04:41.541 --> 00:04:44.280
And psychotherapies often
take 8 to 20 weeks.

00:04:45.435 --> 00:04:48.340
Pharmacotherapy to get a full
response usually takes

00:04:48.341 --> 00:04:50.990
several months and
then people have to be on it.

00:04:50.991 --> 00:04:53.754
So when we think about digital
mental health, we're not talking

00:04:53.755 --> 00:04:55.725
about people using an app for
a couple of days.

00:04:55.726 --> 00:04:59.080
This is something that needs
to be sustained over time.

00:04:59.081 --> 00:05:01.230
It's challenging and it's hard.

00:05:01.231 --> 00:05:02.600
When people are depressed,

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:05.080
they don't have any
motivation to do anything.

00:05:05.081 --> 00:05:06.499
They feel like nothing
is gonna help.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.240
For people with anxiety,
they become fearful and

00:05:10.241 --> 00:05:12.640
they think, this is not gonna
work, this is not gonna work.

00:05:12.641 --> 00:05:14.761
And so
its hard to keep people on task.

00:05:14.762 --> 00:05:19.526
And so if you think about
people using an app over time,

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:24.402
these are gonna be really
challenging to achieve that.

00:05:24.403 --> 00:05:27.353
And so what we end up with
in psychotherapies or

00:05:27.354 --> 00:05:30.986
in treatments generally is we
have specific factors like

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:35.224
teaching people to monitor their
thoughts, or teaching people to

00:05:35.225 --> 00:05:39.330
change their behaviors, those
are kinda the specific skills.

00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:41.890
And then we have what we call
nonspecific factors, like

00:05:41.891 --> 00:05:44.790
empathy, warmth, instillation
of hope that are common

00:05:44.791 --> 00:05:48.140
to all treatments that try to
keep people engaged, okay?

00:05:48.141 --> 00:05:50.840
So this is just sort of
features to keep in mind

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:55.780
from psychotherapy that
might be applicable.

00:05:55.781 --> 00:06:03.020
So just a brief history
of digital mental health.

00:06:03.021 --> 00:06:06.557
People started playing with
this about 20 years ago as

00:06:06.558 --> 00:06:10.870
the Internet became ubiquitous,
and people developed websites.

00:06:10.871 --> 00:06:13.950
The idea was to take
psychotherapy and

00:06:13.951 --> 00:06:15.199
deliver it through a new medium.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.980
And so

00:06:17.981 --> 00:06:21.750
they took some of the components
like in psychotherapy.

00:06:21.751 --> 00:06:23.450
Therapists teach
patients things, right?

00:06:23.451 --> 00:06:25.280
Patients come in,
they don't know some things,

00:06:25.281 --> 00:06:26.570
the therapist teach them things.

00:06:26.571 --> 00:06:28.620
And so some of the websites,

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.520
this is an old website
that we've taken down.

00:06:30.521 --> 00:06:36.540
But just didactics via video and
via text.

00:06:36.541 --> 00:06:40.170
A lot of forms of
psychotherapy have homework,

00:06:40.171 --> 00:06:41.812
sort of work sheets.

00:06:41.813 --> 00:06:44.322
This is a common one for CBT.

00:06:44.323 --> 00:06:46.580
And so those get instantiated
in the websites.

00:06:46.581 --> 00:06:49.503
And then what happened was we
started to build these things

00:06:49.504 --> 00:06:50.730
and nobody would use them.

00:06:50.731 --> 00:06:53.830
So what we did was we put
coaches in to try to get people

00:06:53.831 --> 00:06:55.480
to stay on board.

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:56.380
And so

00:06:56.381 --> 00:06:59.230
there were dashboards that kinda
helped coaches understand what

00:06:59.231 --> 00:07:01.940
the patients were doing to help
them keep people on board.

00:07:01.941 --> 00:07:05.430
Okay, so this is kind of
the standard mental health

00:07:05.431 --> 00:07:07.110
digital mental health.

00:07:07.111 --> 00:07:10.299
And there are some people doing
more interesting things, but

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.364
if you look at the commercial
world this is pretty much where

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:14.113
we still are.

00:07:14.114 --> 00:07:16.889
If you think about
Marshal McClellan said that

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.026
in any new medium,

00:07:18.027 --> 00:07:22.120
the first instance of that new
medium is imitating the old one.

00:07:22.121 --> 00:07:27.260
And that's what we've done here
is we've imitated the old medium

00:07:27.261 --> 00:07:31.591
of face to face hypotherapy,
at least we've tried to.

00:07:31.592 --> 00:07:32.983
And that's taken us a long way.

00:07:32.984 --> 00:07:35.642
There have been more than
a hundred randomized controlled

00:07:35.643 --> 00:07:36.242
trials now.

00:07:36.243 --> 00:07:40.562
And they've pretty consistently
shown that if you just offer it

00:07:40.563 --> 00:07:44.324
without any kind of support,
you get a minimal effect.

00:07:44.325 --> 00:07:45.540
It's not very big.

00:07:45.541 --> 00:07:46.980
People tend not to use it.

00:07:46.981 --> 00:07:48.410
They tend not to stay engaged.

00:07:48.411 --> 00:07:49.670
But if you add in a coach,

00:07:49.671 --> 00:07:52.737
you get effect sizes that start
to look like psychotherapy.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:55.780
And they start to look
like pharmacotherapy.

00:07:55.781 --> 00:07:58.610
So you can get some pretty
robust effect sizes.

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:01.725
And your coaches don't
need to be therapists.

00:08:01.726 --> 00:08:04.648
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:08:04.649 --> 00:08:05.941
human coaches right now.

00:08:05.942 --> 00:08:07.456
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Right,

00:08:07.457 --> 00:08:11.785
people have done with email,
messaging,

00:08:11.786 --> 00:08:17.445
phone calls, sometimes more
times, sometimes less.

00:08:17.446 --> 00:08:20.751
So there's a lot variability in
terms of what that coaching is

00:08:20.752 --> 00:08:24.249
and we'll get there, but I think
one of the problem is we haven't

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.850
really defined what that is.

00:08:25.851 --> 00:08:29.562
But this then,
we have a big research database.

00:08:29.563 --> 00:08:32.207
And then when you look
at implementation,

00:08:32.208 --> 00:08:35.366
as we've started talking
to Kaiser Permanente and

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:38.304
large care systems around
the United States and

00:08:38.305 --> 00:08:42.049
indeed around the world we see,
I have not seen yet one single

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.438
successful implementation
inside a care center yet.

00:08:45.439 --> 00:08:48.129
They may be our there but
I haven't seen it, and

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:50.800
I've seen a lot of failures.

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:55.880
And so we have a huge research
to practice gap here,

00:08:55.881 --> 00:09:00.150
which is not uncommon
in medicine.

00:09:00.151 --> 00:09:03.080
But I think that this
means that there's some

00:09:03.081 --> 00:09:05.800
fundamental problems in
how we've approached this.

00:09:05.801 --> 00:09:08.160
And there are kind
of a lot of them and

00:09:08.161 --> 00:09:10.380
we outlined some of them
in this paper here.

00:09:10.381 --> 00:09:12.010
I'm gonna talk about
two of them here.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.988
One is I think the design
has been atrocious, and

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:19.160
I'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:09:19.161 --> 00:09:21.560
And then the other is we've
ignored the human side, and

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:22.690
I'm not talking about here.

00:09:22.691 --> 00:09:24.660
I'm not talking about putting
apps up on the app store.

00:09:24.661 --> 00:09:25.870
That's perfectly useful.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.582
We've had self-help books for
decades in the stores, and

00:09:30.583 --> 00:09:31.965
people can get those.

00:09:31.966 --> 00:09:34.185
But I'm talking about
designing systems or

00:09:34.186 --> 00:09:37.115
tools that can be embedded
in our healthcare system

00:09:37.116 --> 00:09:40.195
which is where I think we're
gonna actually make an impact.

00:09:40.196 --> 00:09:43.785
So we've ignored the systems and
the human side of it.

00:09:44.785 --> 00:09:48.705
So the design problems, Beating
the Blues is one of the oldest,

00:09:48.706 --> 00:09:53.230
and still I think it's still
commercially available.

00:09:53.231 --> 00:09:56.050
And it's basically a 50-minute
session, psychoeducational

00:09:56.051 --> 00:10:00.570
sessions, that teach CBT
skills via text and video.

00:10:00.571 --> 00:10:04.440
It's not badly designed,
but when you see

00:10:04.441 --> 00:10:06.950
the implementations,
people don't use it.

00:10:06.951 --> 00:10:09.478
When people are depressed,
they don't wanna go home and

00:10:09.479 --> 00:10:11.646
watch a video,
50-minute video every week.

00:10:11.647 --> 00:10:13.140
You can't get people to do it.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:15.472
That, I think, is the problem.

00:10:15.473 --> 00:10:18.881
And there are others that maybe
this is an extreme example, but

00:10:18.882 --> 00:10:21.210
a lot of them have this
kind of flavor to it.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.910
So we've developed products
that people don't use.

00:10:26.911 --> 00:10:28.773
And then I think what
we do is we go out and

00:10:28.774 --> 00:10:30.931
we recruit 400 people
from the Internet.

00:10:30.932 --> 00:10:34.286
And we find the 400 people who
are willing to use the crap that

00:10:34.287 --> 00:10:36.311
we've built,
excuse my French, and

00:10:36.312 --> 00:10:38.160
then we call that the evidence.

00:10:38.161 --> 00:10:41.270
So it's not surprising
that we can't get

00:10:41.271 --> 00:10:43.060
the stuff to work
in the real world.

00:10:43.061 --> 00:10:44.472
The development's been top down.

00:10:44.473 --> 00:10:46.780
It's been people like me
deciding what patients

00:10:46.781 --> 00:10:47.880
need to have.

00:10:47.881 --> 00:10:50.160
And then we get that to them,
and

00:10:50.161 --> 00:10:54.053
then we try to push them to
do what we think they should.

00:10:54.054 --> 00:10:56.800
And it hasn't fit into
the fabric of people's lives.

00:10:56.801 --> 00:11:01.970
So one of the challenges that
we've been thinking about

00:11:01.971 --> 00:11:06.860
in terms of starting to deliver
tools digitally to people is in

00:11:06.861 --> 00:11:09.980
mental health, we have lots and
lots of behavioral strategies.

00:11:09.981 --> 00:11:14.680
So when a person has depression,
you don't treat depression.

00:11:14.681 --> 00:11:17.221
You don't say,
we're gonna fix your depression.

00:11:17.222 --> 00:11:20.029
Ultimately, what we're talking
about is little components of

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:20.969
treatment, right?

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:24.200
So we have behavioral
activation.

00:11:24.201 --> 00:11:25.570
So we try to get
people to out and

00:11:25.571 --> 00:11:27.420
do more positive stuff, right?

00:11:27.421 --> 00:11:30.855
We have cognitive restructuring
to try to help people recognize

00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:32.004
negative thoughts.

00:11:32.005 --> 00:11:34.935
So we have goal setting,
problem solving,

00:11:34.936 --> 00:11:37.390
lots of behavioral strategies.

00:11:37.391 --> 00:11:40.210
And some work for some people,
others work for other people.

00:11:40.211 --> 00:11:41.820
Some people prefer some.

00:11:41.821 --> 00:11:43.130
So how do you structure that?

00:11:43.131 --> 00:11:47.490
And then at the same time,
increasingly, we have

00:11:47.491 --> 00:11:49.890
lots of different features
in the technology, right?

00:11:49.891 --> 00:11:52.640
So just for apps, you've
got text, you've got video,

00:11:52.641 --> 00:11:54.930
you've got audio,
you've got messaging.

00:11:54.931 --> 00:11:59.486
You can do tracking,
you can do logging, feedback.

00:11:59.487 --> 00:12:01.550
There's all kinds of different
ways that people interact.

00:12:01.551 --> 00:12:03.160
Some people like logging things.

00:12:03.161 --> 00:12:04.560
Some people hate logging things.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:10.270
So you can see here that if
we're gonna design something

00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:13.020
that's gonna help the vast
majority of people, you're gonna

00:12:13.021 --> 00:12:15.610
be filling in a grid here
that's gonna be enormous.

00:12:15.611 --> 00:12:19.020
And how do you design a system
that is gonna meet people

00:12:19.021 --> 00:12:21.490
with the interaction styles
that they prefer, and

00:12:21.491 --> 00:12:23.390
the kind of
behavioral strategies?

00:12:23.391 --> 00:12:26.970
And so what we've
started to do is to say,

00:12:26.971 --> 00:12:30.191
we can't develop an app for
this.

00:12:30.192 --> 00:12:36.320
[LAUGH] What we can do,
the way that people use apps or

00:12:36.321 --> 00:12:39.040
tools right now is
the little component parts.

00:12:39.041 --> 00:12:42.310
You don't use an app to
manage your entire life.

00:12:42.311 --> 00:12:44.170
You use an app to
find a restaurant,

00:12:44.171 --> 00:12:45.870
you use a map to map it there.

00:12:45.871 --> 00:12:51.417
You use an app to manage
your plane flight.

00:12:51.418 --> 00:12:54.450
You have little apps that
do little things, right?

00:12:54.451 --> 00:12:57.210
And so what we've done is
created a suite of apps,

00:12:57.211 --> 00:13:00.232
kind of this is a sort of
a representation that we call in

00:13:00.233 --> 00:13:02.690
IntelliCare, and
it's 13 or 14 apps.

00:13:02.691 --> 00:13:06.210
And each one is just a single
behavioral strategy and

00:13:06.211 --> 00:13:07.720
a single interaction style.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:15.370
And so, the goal is to
get something that for

00:13:15.371 --> 00:13:17.420
the most part,
can be used pretty quickly.

00:13:17.421 --> 00:13:20.130
People can open it,
they can use it, and

00:13:20.131 --> 00:13:21.130
then they're done with it.

00:13:21.131 --> 00:13:23.920
And then there's one app
that we call the hub app,

00:13:23.921 --> 00:13:26.590
that if people download that,

00:13:26.591 --> 00:13:30.580
it organizes their interaction
with the whole suite, right?

00:13:30.581 --> 00:13:33.430
So it's just breaking it down
into the component parts, so

00:13:33.431 --> 00:13:36.810
they can be flexibly applied
to people as needed.

00:13:36.811 --> 00:13:38.233
Everybody with me?

00:13:38.234 --> 00:13:42.230
Okay, so
this is just an example.

00:13:42.231 --> 00:13:44.180
So Day to Day,
I'll give you some examples.

00:13:44.181 --> 00:13:47.010
These are all up on Google Play,
and

00:13:47.011 --> 00:13:51.780
I think the first three of
four of them are up on iOS.

00:13:51.781 --> 00:13:55.835
So Day to Day is more didactic,
kind of like those old ones.

00:13:55.836 --> 00:14:00.153
So it'll give a brief sort of
didactic explanation each week,

00:14:00.154 --> 00:14:04.633
and then give reminders, sort of
messages every day to kinda keep

00:14:04.634 --> 00:14:08.220
people reminded of that
sort of lesson, right?

00:14:08.221 --> 00:14:10.225
So this is kind of traditional.

00:14:10.226 --> 00:14:12.770
This is sort of a traditional
Thought Challenger thing.

00:14:12.771 --> 00:14:17.624
So you go in, you can can
identify a negative thought, and

00:14:17.625 --> 00:14:20.509
then find a way of
challenging it.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.563
But then there are others like
Daily Feats is really pretty

00:14:23.564 --> 00:14:24.070
simple.

00:14:24.071 --> 00:14:26.730
You get in, you peg your
level of depression.

00:14:26.731 --> 00:14:29.320
If you're really depressed,
you start at Level 1.

00:14:29.321 --> 00:14:32.590
And I got out of bed, I groomed
myself, I made good food

00:14:32.591 --> 00:14:35.440
choices, or I ate something,
and I went outside.

00:14:35.441 --> 00:14:36.550
That's it.

00:14:36.551 --> 00:14:38.842
And you check those
off each day, four or

00:14:38.843 --> 00:14:41.660
five times in a row, and
then you level up to Level 2,

00:14:41.661 --> 00:14:43.630
and you get another set.

00:14:43.631 --> 00:14:47.520
You can add some in if you want,
takes ten seconds, that's it.

00:14:47.521 --> 00:14:50.917
This app has actually been the
most popular app, cuz it's so

00:14:50.918 --> 00:14:51.458
simple.

00:14:51.459 --> 00:14:54.243
And so I thought,
nobody's ever gonna use this,

00:14:54.244 --> 00:14:57.450
when it was one of my
developers thought it up.

00:14:57.451 --> 00:15:00.880
This one gets used more than
any of them cuz it's simple.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.440
This one uses images
a little bit more.

00:15:04.441 --> 00:15:08.600
So you can take a photo, add
a mantra to it, and it loads and

00:15:08.601 --> 00:15:10.710
it reminds you of that
mantra periodically.

00:15:10.711 --> 00:15:13.650
That's all it does.

00:15:13.651 --> 00:15:17.570
So we've had, since the last
two years from Google Play,

00:15:17.571 --> 00:15:22.250
we've had about 80,000 installs,
76,000 unique users.

00:15:22.251 --> 00:15:27.150
And as of a couple of days ago,
there were 10,000 active users,

00:15:27.151 --> 00:15:31.340
meaning that they've used one of
the apps over the last 30 days.

00:15:31.341 --> 00:15:33.503
So it's been working
pretty well.

00:15:33.504 --> 00:15:37.313
If we've looked at a benchmark,
one benchmark that's been used

00:15:37.314 --> 00:15:40.230
for health apps is,
most people download an app.

00:15:40.231 --> 00:15:42.960
And you maybe open it once and
never look at it again.

00:15:42.961 --> 00:15:46.105
So this was a diet app, and

00:15:46.106 --> 00:15:49.660
2.6% of people used it
more than 10 times.

00:15:49.661 --> 00:15:54.640
So by that, around 5 to 35%
of the people that have

00:15:54.641 --> 00:15:58.370
downloaded an IntelliCare
app have met that criteria.

00:15:58.371 --> 00:16:02.140
So you can see here,
across all the apps on average,

00:16:02.141 --> 00:16:05.360
they're used about 13 times
over the course of a month.

00:16:05.361 --> 00:16:10.157
The Daily Feats, our top one
is used 27 times over 45

00:16:10.158 --> 00:16:14.152
days on average,
Day to day, over 38 days.

00:16:14.153 --> 00:16:16.957
And then, all the way down here,
this one is so much of a dog,

00:16:16.958 --> 00:16:18.010
we've pulled it off.

00:16:18.011 --> 00:16:20.000
We haven't been able to fix it.

00:16:20.001 --> 00:16:25.583
So some of these others
here need more help.

00:16:25.584 --> 00:16:29.526
And I should also say,
we've been iterating over these,

00:16:29.527 --> 00:16:31.707
after we've put them up there.

00:16:31.708 --> 00:16:34.077
We get people to give
us feedback, and

00:16:34.078 --> 00:16:36.680
we made changes until
we ran out of money.

00:16:36.681 --> 00:16:39.582
And now they're up
there the way they are.

00:16:39.583 --> 00:16:42.558
So, we also Have done a trial.

00:16:42.559 --> 00:16:44.622
This is, so
we've done two trials now,

00:16:44.623 --> 00:16:46.270
I'll talk about the first one.

00:16:46.271 --> 00:16:48.610
The second one, we're just
starting to analyze the data.

00:16:48.611 --> 00:16:52.000
So this is just the first
field trial with 100 people.

00:16:52.001 --> 00:16:55.180
And so this was starting to look
at how do you put this into

00:16:55.181 --> 00:16:56.960
a care system with coaching?

00:16:56.961 --> 00:17:03.620
And so we enrolled 99 people,
who had the PHQ

00:17:03.621 --> 00:17:07.180
as a measure of depression and
the GID as a measure of anxiety.

00:17:07.181 --> 00:17:09.020
So, these are people
with elevated levels,

00:17:09.021 --> 00:17:10.010
levels of depression and

00:17:10.011 --> 00:17:12.860
anxiety where physicians would
refer them for treatment.

00:17:12.861 --> 00:17:15.270
And so this is the dashboard.

00:17:15.271 --> 00:17:19.780
And there's, this here is the
apps, when they've been used and

00:17:19.781 --> 00:17:22.520
launched, and if the care
manager clicks on it,

00:17:22.521 --> 00:17:24.550
it gives them
a little information.

00:17:24.551 --> 00:17:27.820
This is,
manages the text messaging.

00:17:27.821 --> 00:17:30.420
This is the apps that
are installed and the apps that

00:17:30.421 --> 00:17:34.770
are being used and then this
is their sort of history of

00:17:34.771 --> 00:17:37.940
symptom severity, so this is
all that the coaches get.

00:17:37.941 --> 00:17:40.630
And they had a 30 minute
phone call with people to get

00:17:40.631 --> 00:17:42.430
started and
then over the eight weeks,

00:17:42.431 --> 00:17:45.110
they just used text
messaging back and forth.

00:17:45.111 --> 00:17:47.650
And so this is the reduction
in depression and

00:17:47.651 --> 00:17:49.221
anxiety that we saw.

00:17:49.222 --> 00:17:53.208
So it's substantial,
viewed another way, for

00:17:53.209 --> 00:17:56.808
depression,everybody started
out blue,

00:17:56.809 --> 00:18:01.080
so they all had significant
levels of depression.

00:18:01.081 --> 00:18:04.122
And at the end of the eight
weeks 37% had no symptoms of

00:18:04.123 --> 00:18:05.715
depression at all anymore.

00:18:05.716 --> 00:18:09.120
40% had dropped
below the threshold,

00:18:09.121 --> 00:18:11.210
there still have some
residual symptoms.

00:18:11.211 --> 00:18:14.660
And 22% still had symptoms
that needed treatment.

00:18:14.661 --> 00:18:18.179
So these are good findings
we were please with this.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:24.430
And we've currently just
finished randomized trial and

00:18:24.431 --> 00:18:26.390
the outcomes are looking
very similar.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.170
So how might this work?

00:18:30.171 --> 00:18:31.670
Why might this be working?

00:18:31.671 --> 00:18:35.520
Well I mean at a simple level,
people are using the app.

00:18:35.521 --> 00:18:38.862
So maybe that's helping, and
they're getting coaching so

00:18:38.863 --> 00:18:40.572
those are the two pieces here.

00:18:40.573 --> 00:18:43.940
So let's look at the app use.

00:18:43.941 --> 00:18:46.920
So the average app launch
is over eight weeks

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.060
were 195 app launches.

00:18:51.061 --> 00:18:53.900
And if you know,

00:18:53.901 --> 00:18:57.530
if you're familiar with
kind of that's a lot.

00:18:57.531 --> 00:19:00.610
That and typically when you-
&gt;&gt; Per person?

00:19:00.611 --> 00:19:01.580
&gt;&gt; What?
&gt;&gt; Per person?

00:19:01.581 --> 00:19:05.030
&gt;&gt; Per person,
over eight weeks, yeah.

00:19:05.031 --> 00:19:06.960
And just in those eight weeks.

00:19:06.961 --> 00:19:09.130
And when you look at app use for
these interventions,

00:19:09.131 --> 00:19:11.958
typically you get kind of
a hockey stick, right?

00:19:11.959 --> 00:19:15.250
You get people,
they start off using it and

00:19:15.251 --> 00:19:18.590
then in the first week or two
they drop off and it gets low.

00:19:18.591 --> 00:19:21.170
Here what we see is people
are consistently using three or

00:19:21.171 --> 00:19:24.020
four, launching
the apps three or

00:19:24.021 --> 00:19:26.440
four times a day throughout
the entire trial.

00:19:26.441 --> 00:19:29.310
And in fact two-thirds of
them continue to use them

00:19:29.311 --> 00:19:30.750
after the trial ended.

00:19:30.751 --> 00:19:35.258
And so I think the reason
that we see that,

00:19:35.259 --> 00:19:40.460
there's, one is that the average
time it takes is a minute and

00:19:40.461 --> 00:19:41.900
the median is 17 seconds.

00:19:41.901 --> 00:19:45.690
So we have a bunch of apps
that are like the checklist,

00:19:45.691 --> 00:19:48.150
they just take a few seconds
to use, they're simple.

00:19:48.151 --> 00:19:50.715
And there are a couple
that are longer,

00:19:50.716 --> 00:19:53.049
like there's some relaxation, so

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:57.350
that it takes five minutes, ten
minutes depending on the video.

00:19:57.351 --> 00:19:59.590
But in general they
are simple to use, so

00:19:59.591 --> 00:20:02.630
they can fit easily into
the fabric of people's lives.

00:20:02.631 --> 00:20:05.360
The other I think is that the
idea and I hadn't thought of of

00:20:05.361 --> 00:20:08.350
this when we designed it, but
people are getting new apps.

00:20:08.351 --> 00:20:10.360
They use the apps for
a couple, a week or two and

00:20:10.361 --> 00:20:11.460
then they use another one.

00:20:11.461 --> 00:20:13.820
And so it's not like
staying with the same app,

00:20:13.821 --> 00:20:15.330
there's novelty involved.

00:20:15.331 --> 00:20:17.870
And so they're not
necessarily getting sick of

00:20:17.871 --> 00:20:19.630
doing the same thing over and
over again.

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:23.320
So then I wanna go back and
talk about you.

00:20:23.321 --> 00:20:24.730
So you've already
seen these data,

00:20:24.731 --> 00:20:26.209
this is from
the public deployment.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.855
So this is fresh data, so

00:20:29.856 --> 00:20:34.876
it's not exactly comparable but
you can see here that for

00:20:34.877 --> 00:20:39.895
the apps, the mean here is
27 in the, with the coaching

00:20:39.896 --> 00:20:44.830
it was mean at 29 so it's
not exactly the same metric.

00:20:44.831 --> 00:20:46.111
But it's sort of in the,

00:20:46.112 --> 00:20:48.616
you can see this is in
the same ballpark, right.

00:20:48.617 --> 00:20:52.070
So the use patterns once
somebody has it on their phone

00:20:52.071 --> 00:20:55.370
the use pattern within any
individual app is kind of

00:20:55.371 --> 00:20:57.380
the same which is surprising.

00:20:57.381 --> 00:20:59.000
So the coaches aren't
necessarily getting,

00:20:59.001 --> 00:21:01.170
once the app's on their
phone the coaches aren't

00:21:01.171 --> 00:21:04.380
really getting them to
use it more necessarily.

00:21:04.381 --> 00:21:07.410
But what you see up here

00:21:07.411 --> 00:21:11.280
is that people are using
more of the apps.

00:21:11.281 --> 00:21:16.223
The coach is really able to get
them to use more of the apps.

00:21:16.224 --> 00:21:20.306
I think in the public deployment
two-thirds of the people only

00:21:20.307 --> 00:21:21.400
use one app.

00:21:21.401 --> 00:21:24.390
And one-third then start
using a number of them.

00:21:24.391 --> 00:21:27.480
And it's mostly the people
who download the Hub app

00:21:27.481 --> 00:21:29.820
who end up using more of them
cuz they get recommendations.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:37.750
So recommendations maybe
increase app use and

00:21:37.751 --> 00:21:43.500
this is just the data from the
first people with the Hub app on

00:21:43.501 --> 00:21:48.450
their phone, and they get, they
end up for these different apps

00:21:48.451 --> 00:21:51.610
they end up, if a recommendation
automatically is launched it

00:21:51.611 --> 00:21:54.860
increases the likelihood that
they're going to use that app by

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:57.440
this amount, yeah?

00:21:57.441 --> 00:22:01.630
&gt;&gt; So your individual apps don't
recommend any of the other apps?

00:22:01.631 --> 00:22:02.869
Just the Hub recommends the?

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:03.932
&gt;&gt; It all goes through the hub.

00:22:03.933 --> 00:22:07.060
And so the Hub,
thank you for asking that.

00:22:07.061 --> 00:22:11.969
So what the Hub does is it
monitors what's on the phone and

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:15.139
then it will make
a recommendation,

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:18.427
each week it'll
recommend a new app.

00:22:18.428 --> 00:22:22.224
Initially we just did it
randomly and we've recently put

00:22:22.225 --> 00:22:26.418
in just a commodity recommender
engine that increases the, so

00:22:26.419 --> 00:22:29.520
then I'll show you,
it increases slightly.

00:22:29.521 --> 00:22:31.990
But we think that where we're
going with this is we think that

00:22:31.991 --> 00:22:34.460
if we can start to learn how
to recommend it, we can start

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:40.530
to increase the helpfulness
of those recommendations.

00:22:40.531 --> 00:22:43.639
&gt;&gt; Was there a reason that you
don't have each individual app

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.246
all at the end say, and
check out our other apps?

00:22:48.999 --> 00:22:51.540
&gt;&gt; No, there's no good reasons
for why we did it that way.

00:22:51.541 --> 00:22:54.450
Honestly, I think we were just
trying to figure out how to get

00:22:54.451 --> 00:22:56.720
this out and launched.

00:22:56.721 --> 00:23:01.410
So we didn't have we had the Hub
app do a number of things.

00:23:01.411 --> 00:23:07.180
Once that's, once the Hub app
is on the phone, it manages

00:23:07.181 --> 00:23:11.420
the recommendations, it then
manages all the notifications,

00:23:11.421 --> 00:23:14.850
so it kind of everything then
goes through the Hub app.

00:23:14.851 --> 00:23:18.293
It also, I think it's probably
need, entire need of some

00:23:18.294 --> 00:23:21.882
redesign because it's the thing
that people complain about

00:23:21.883 --> 00:23:28.836
the most, so it's not
the best way to do it So,

00:23:28.837 --> 00:23:33.788
but along those lines,
when we look at these apps,

00:23:33.789 --> 00:23:38.930
looking at app use,
they fall into clusters.

00:23:38.931 --> 00:23:42.470
So people who use day to day or
iCOPE or MyMantra,

00:23:42.471 --> 00:23:45.930
if they use one of those they're
more likely to use others right.

00:23:45.931 --> 00:23:47.340
And they fall into,

00:23:47.341 --> 00:23:50.465
I think, four of these
seem to make sense.

00:23:50.466 --> 00:23:55.070
So, the first column there is
language or they use sort of,

00:23:55.071 --> 00:23:57.580
seems like they're engaging
people cognitively, so

00:23:57.581 --> 00:24:01.160
it's sort of a psychological
method of engagement.

00:24:01.161 --> 00:24:04.210
The second one, The Purple Chill
is the relaxation app.

00:24:04.211 --> 00:24:07.710
Slumber Time is sleep helps
people with insomnia.

00:24:07.711 --> 00:24:09.820
So, kind of relaxation apps,

00:24:09.821 --> 00:24:12.520
then check-it apps that rely
on checklists so that's more,

00:24:12.521 --> 00:24:16.320
kind of, interaction style that
seems to be holding it together.

00:24:16.321 --> 00:24:19.900
Activity Boost Me is kind
of behavioral activation so

00:24:19.901 --> 00:24:22.350
getting people to do
more positive activities.

00:24:22.351 --> 00:24:25.480
Move Me is getting people to
do more physical activity.

00:24:25.481 --> 00:24:26.790
So those kinda hang together.

00:24:26.791 --> 00:24:27.786
So these make sense, and

00:24:27.787 --> 00:24:30.264
these are just the worst apps
that we have, and I'm not sure.

00:24:33.842 --> 00:24:37.936
I'm not sure analytically
what's happening, but

00:24:37.937 --> 00:24:39.896
they're our junky app.

00:24:39.897 --> 00:24:44.536
So you can see that, I think we
can start thinking about having

00:24:44.537 --> 00:24:47.392
a recommender engine
that can drive,

00:24:47.393 --> 00:24:52.301
essentially be kind of a Netflix
for mental health apps, that can

00:24:52.302 --> 00:24:57.230
start directing people to apps
that they're more likely to use.

00:24:57.231 --> 00:24:58.179
And we're, so

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:01.836
we're doing this in the context
of Intellicare right now.

00:25:01.837 --> 00:25:04.716
So we're working with
some people at Columbia,

00:25:04.717 --> 00:25:06.333
some biostatisticians and

00:25:06.334 --> 00:25:09.284
machine learning people
at Columbia who are doing

00:25:09.285 --> 00:25:12.670
the analytics to try to develop
a better system for this.

00:25:12.671 --> 00:25:15.740
But you can also imagine that
this kind of analytic system

00:25:15.741 --> 00:25:17.350
could be used for any app.

00:25:17.351 --> 00:25:18.860
They don't necessarily
have to be ours.

00:25:18.861 --> 00:25:22.550
But you can start to have
a platform that can begin to

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:27.630
provide guidance to people for
the, I don't know how many,

00:25:27.631 --> 00:25:30.310
hundreds of thousands of mental
health apps that are out there

00:25:30.311 --> 00:25:31.910
on the app stores.

00:25:31.911 --> 00:25:34.860
So that's one direction
that we're going with

00:25:34.861 --> 00:25:37.830
the design here.

00:25:37.831 --> 00:25:40.610
Maybe we don't really need care
managers if we can get people to

00:25:40.611 --> 00:25:41.520
use the apps, right?

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:44.680
And I still think we do.

00:25:44.681 --> 00:25:46.720
And there's two reasons.

00:25:46.721 --> 00:25:50.200
One is, I don't think health
care settings are gonna want to

00:25:50.201 --> 00:25:52.450
host apps as a form
of treatment.

00:25:52.451 --> 00:25:56.122
They're in the business
of providing services.

00:25:56.123 --> 00:26:00.612
So I think that we have to start
thinking about how we can use

00:26:00.613 --> 00:26:05.013
digital technologies to improve
their ability to extend

00:26:05.014 --> 00:26:06.130
services.

00:26:06.131 --> 00:26:09.300
And then the other is
that I think we still,

00:26:09.301 --> 00:26:11.850
in the work that we've done, we
have some biased samples, right?

00:26:11.851 --> 00:26:13.600
We're recruiting people
who wanna come in and

00:26:13.601 --> 00:26:14.420
be part of a study.

00:26:14.421 --> 00:26:15.760
These aren't your
average patients.

00:26:15.761 --> 00:26:18.530
These are people that just the
fact that they're in our study,

00:26:18.531 --> 00:26:21.050
they've already demonstrated
that they're more motivated than

00:26:21.051 --> 00:26:22.160
the average person.

00:26:22.161 --> 00:26:24.780
That's pretty different than
having a care manager say,

00:26:24.781 --> 00:26:26.400
I think you should do x,
y, or z.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:28.170
That's gonna be,

00:26:28.171 --> 00:26:30.240
that you're gonna get
a whole different level.

00:26:30.241 --> 00:26:33.180
We know that patients,
50% of patients don't even

00:26:33.181 --> 00:26:36.240
take the medications that
their doctors recommend them.

00:26:36.241 --> 00:26:40.720
So we're dealing with, I think
there's still a huge role for

00:26:40.721 --> 00:26:44.400
care management even though it
looks like we're doing well.

00:26:45.405 --> 00:26:48.184
So, I describe
the coaching that we did.

00:26:48.185 --> 00:26:53.790
30 to 40 minutes of call time,
20, 25 text messages.

00:26:53.791 --> 00:26:59.440
And when we look at the text
messages, they fall into 10 or

00:26:59.441 --> 00:27:02.320
15 different buckets
when we cluster them.

00:27:02.321 --> 00:27:04.370
One is, opinions about the apps.

00:27:04.371 --> 00:27:06.240
I like this, I don't like it.

00:27:06.241 --> 00:27:10.360
Picking which app to use, making
a decision, technical problems;

00:27:10.361 --> 00:27:14.192
sorry, responding to the coach,
sorry, I didn't get back to you.

00:27:14.193 --> 00:27:16.860
Excuses, scheduling, etcetera.

00:27:16.861 --> 00:27:22.520
Things are going well, yes,
no, and thank you, right?

00:27:22.521 --> 00:27:25.110
These are pretty well
defined buckets.

00:27:25.111 --> 00:27:29.150
When we start looking
at the coaching.

00:27:29.151 --> 00:27:31.820
A lot of this could be
automated, I think.

00:27:31.821 --> 00:27:34.900
I don't think care systems
at this point are ready to

00:27:34.901 --> 00:27:37.380
turn it all over to
something that's automated.

00:27:37.381 --> 00:27:40.410
But, and you'll see in a minute,

00:27:40.411 --> 00:27:44.080
I think providing automation
can really help care managers.

00:27:44.081 --> 00:27:45.540
Cuz care managers
in these systems

00:27:45.541 --> 00:27:48.370
are not necessarily trained in
managing mental health problems.

00:27:48.371 --> 00:27:48.870
Yeah?

00:27:48.871 --> 00:27:52.070
&gt;&gt; So if those
are the general buckets,

00:27:52.071 --> 00:27:56.000
do you get ones that are like, I
can't go on today, please help?

00:27:56.001 --> 00:28:00.980
Meaning calls for
help type thing?

00:28:00.981 --> 00:28:01.840
&gt;&gt; I think we will.

00:28:01.841 --> 00:28:04.780
So we, in these early studies,

00:28:04.781 --> 00:28:07.210
we screen people out with
significant suicidality.

00:28:07.211 --> 00:28:10.700
There are people who
have some suicidality, so

00:28:10.701 --> 00:28:14.330
they certainly Having suicidal
ideation is part of it, and

00:28:14.331 --> 00:28:18.990
we do get some people who talk
about those who'll raise it.

00:28:18.991 --> 00:28:21.740
We did not have any of those
hundred people say I'm gonna go

00:28:21.741 --> 00:28:23.240
out and kill myself.

00:28:23.241 --> 00:28:24.840
Which does happen in
some of my studies.

00:28:24.841 --> 00:28:28.170
So then we call them up and
we usually talk with them and

00:28:28.171 --> 00:28:29.550
access them.

00:28:29.551 --> 00:28:31.990
&gt;&gt; That's interesting, I'm also
curious about just in general

00:28:31.991 --> 00:28:34.170
&gt;&gt; Maybe not suicidal just I'm

00:28:34.171 --> 00:28:37.710
having so much stress about my
job tomorrow, not suicidal but

00:28:37.711 --> 00:28:42.320
just needing more help as far
as dealing with anxiety or

00:28:42.321 --> 00:28:43.390
calls for help in that sense.

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:49.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean part of, so
you know part, what I haven't

00:28:49.941 --> 00:28:52.930
presented here is kind of
the development of our care

00:28:52.931 --> 00:28:57.100
management model and we also
didn't get that right first.

00:28:57.101 --> 00:29:00.990
When we started out,
as we were going through and

00:29:00.991 --> 00:29:03.590
talking to the users and
the care managers.

00:29:03.591 --> 00:29:06.510
There was a lot of lack of
clarity of what the role

00:29:06.511 --> 00:29:07.520
of the care manager was.

00:29:07.521 --> 00:29:11.175
So one of the things we had to
do Is make that pretty explicit

00:29:11.176 --> 00:29:13.346
up front that this
is not therapy.

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:19.099
And still people brought
up things like a death in

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:23.240
the family or
significant stressors.

00:29:23.241 --> 00:29:28.550
And so, when those came
up If it was significant

00:29:28.551 --> 00:29:33.600
enough that they could arrange
for a second call to talk with

00:29:33.601 --> 00:29:36.170
them for a few minutes to try to
connect them with other services

00:29:36.171 --> 00:29:38.480
that might be more
appropriate or like that.

00:29:38.481 --> 00:29:42.400
They didn't get into, but the
care managers did not get into

00:29:42.401 --> 00:29:45.080
helping people solve
specific problems.

00:29:45.081 --> 00:29:46.410
They didn't go over the line and

00:29:46.411 --> 00:29:49.430
to starting to do the
psychotherapy in The reason for

00:29:49.431 --> 00:29:53.790
that is that we're anticipating
that the kind of people that

00:29:53.791 --> 00:29:56.680
would manage a system like this,
care managers,

00:29:56.681 --> 00:29:58.770
typically don't have a lot
of mental health training.

00:29:58.771 --> 00:30:03.130
So, you're gonna need
a system that have

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:08.090
a defined set of goals for
those managers.

00:30:08.091 --> 00:30:09.740
Does that kind of answer your?

00:30:09.741 --> 00:30:10.270
&gt;&gt; It does.

00:30:10.271 --> 00:30:12.430
So it sounds like you've
gotten some of those.

00:30:12.431 --> 00:30:15.130
Are those a lot less than these?

00:30:15.131 --> 00:30:19.360
So you wouldn't assign
a category to those type of

00:30:19.361 --> 00:30:19.920
incoming texts?

00:30:19.921 --> 00:30:20.800
&gt;&gt; I would have to go back.

00:30:20.801 --> 00:30:23.540
So these we just
got from somebody

00:30:23.541 --> 00:30:27.210
that did our NLP at University
of Washington yesterday, so

00:30:27.211 --> 00:30:29.310
I put these in last night.

00:30:29.311 --> 00:30:34.050
So I don't [LAUGH] I don't
actually know what happened to

00:30:34.051 --> 00:30:37.980
those sort of random ones that
didn't fit in everywhere.

00:30:37.981 --> 00:30:40.425
So, sorry.

00:30:40.426 --> 00:30:43.480
[LAUGH] But I think that's

00:30:43.481 --> 00:30:46.980
when we anticipate kind of how
a system like this might work.

00:30:46.981 --> 00:30:47.960
What I anticipate is,

00:30:47.961 --> 00:30:50.910
I don't think in a care
system right now.

00:30:50.911 --> 00:30:54.730
They would be comfortable with
any kind of message going out to

00:30:54.731 --> 00:30:58.550
a patient that haven't
been breaded by human.

00:30:58.551 --> 00:31:01.380
I think that means in any
kind of care setting.

00:31:01.381 --> 00:31:04.270
Maybe we'll get there but
I think most care environments

00:31:04.271 --> 00:31:06.740
right now that that's
a step too far.

00:31:06.741 --> 00:31:10.120
But I think what we can
do is give care managers,

00:31:10.121 --> 00:31:13.010
send this message, have them
look at it and send it.

00:31:13.011 --> 00:31:15.970
And then have the ones that
are like you're describing,

00:31:15.971 --> 00:31:18.180
that there isn't
an automatic response.

00:31:18.181 --> 00:31:20.680
Here's something, here
are three different levels, or

00:31:20.681 --> 00:31:24.200
here are three different types
of responses you might give,

00:31:24.201 --> 00:31:28.050
or contact somebody for
further assistance on this.

00:31:28.051 --> 00:31:29.490
Essentially guiding.

00:31:29.491 --> 00:31:32.380
In the same way that we're
trying to give patients

00:31:32.381 --> 00:31:35.640
guidance, I think we have to
give the care managers guidance.

00:31:35.641 --> 00:31:38.380
On of the things we did in
August is I think is what Kale's

00:31:38.381 --> 00:31:41.780
getting at is right off the bat
they have to give us a rating

00:31:41.781 --> 00:31:43.660
on how suicidal they feel.

00:31:43.661 --> 00:31:46.730
And if it goes over a certain
number we have the trigger.

00:31:46.731 --> 00:31:49.260
We know exactly what the
response should be, call 9111.

00:31:49.261 --> 00:31:51.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and you're dealing
with a population there that is

00:31:51.711 --> 00:31:52.860
really very suicidal.

00:31:52.861 --> 00:31:55.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we have to be,
we're always super concerned.

00:31:55.121 --> 00:31:59.422
&gt;&gt; Dealing with suicidality in
the context of primary care,

00:31:59.423 --> 00:32:00.562
has been that,

00:32:00.563 --> 00:32:04.970
each organization we have to
negotiate that differently.

00:32:04.971 --> 00:32:09.323
Because they, obviously when you
go into primary care sort of

00:32:09.324 --> 00:32:10.746
medical settings,

00:32:10.747 --> 00:32:14.960
nobody wants their patients
to commit suicide.

00:32:14.961 --> 00:32:22.070
But physicians want,
they don't have time.

00:32:22.071 --> 00:32:24.540
What they need is,
this is what you need to do.

00:32:24.541 --> 00:32:25.270
Here's a yes/no.

00:32:25.271 --> 00:32:27.980
This person is suicidal,
you need to refer them,

00:32:27.981 --> 00:32:29.220
you need to get them help.

00:32:29.221 --> 00:32:31.440
This person is not.

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:37.490
And it's really difficult in
these systems to get that level

00:32:37.491 --> 00:32:38.550
of Clarity.

00:32:38.551 --> 00:32:39.480
So we end up for

00:32:39.481 --> 00:32:43.340
example, when we were
working with Northwestern,

00:32:43.341 --> 00:32:45.110
one of the outpatient
clinics in Northwestern.

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:47.310
We didn't assess it,

00:32:47.311 --> 00:32:49.380
they did not want us
to assess suicidality.

00:32:49.381 --> 00:32:50.880
They were worried
about liability.

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.600
So we screen them out, we assess
it right at the beginning and

00:32:54.601 --> 00:32:57.320
we just screen people out who
had high levels of suicidality.

00:32:57.321 --> 00:32:59.370
Other places, they do
want that information and

00:32:59.371 --> 00:33:02.610
they have somebody tasked with
who can do that outreach.

00:33:02.611 --> 00:33:07.010
So it's really, I mean again,
this is where, I think a one

00:33:07.011 --> 00:33:11.560
size fits all, is not going
to work in our care system.

00:33:11.561 --> 00:33:13.810
And when you see
one care system,

00:33:13.811 --> 00:33:15.310
you've seen one care system.

00:33:15.311 --> 00:33:18.771
And there's a lot that doesn't
translate to other care systems.

00:33:18.772 --> 00:33:22.256
So I think these kinds of
tools are gonna have to be

00:33:22.257 --> 00:33:25.913
adaptable to the needs of
the individual systems.

00:33:25.914 --> 00:33:30.332
So, what we've done, and
speaking of that, what we

00:33:30.333 --> 00:33:35.160
started doing is thinking,
realizing what we have to do.

00:33:35.161 --> 00:33:38.150
If we're starting to think about
designing tools to go into

00:33:38.151 --> 00:33:41.060
a care system, we have to design
for the patients, obviously.

00:33:41.061 --> 00:33:43.100
We have to design for
whoever's gonna manage it, but

00:33:43.101 --> 00:33:45.200
we also have to design and
understand the care system.

00:33:45.201 --> 00:33:47.598
So we've started, we've just
now starting to work with three

00:33:47.599 --> 00:33:48.600
different care systems.

00:33:48.601 --> 00:33:51.230
One is Northwestern Medicine
Physician Partners,

00:33:51.231 --> 00:33:55.040
which is an arm of
Northwestern Medicine that is

00:33:55.041 --> 00:33:57.090
building out its
value-based care.

00:33:57.091 --> 00:34:01.190
So they take, essentially, Blue
Cross, Blue Shield contracts.

00:34:01.191 --> 00:34:03.290
They don't do fee-for-service.

00:34:03.291 --> 00:34:07.000
And they're buying up practices
from Lake Michigan out to

00:34:07.001 --> 00:34:08.230
the Mississippi River.

00:34:08.231 --> 00:34:13.110
So they're sort of in this
expanding their footprint.

00:34:13.111 --> 00:34:15.490
And they need to be able to
manage mental health problems.

00:34:15.491 --> 00:34:16.760
It's a huge problem for them,

00:34:16.761 --> 00:34:18.080
because they don't
have capacity.

00:34:18.081 --> 00:34:20.440
And if you get out of
the greater Chicago area,

00:34:20.441 --> 00:34:22.500
there aren't any providers.

00:34:22.501 --> 00:34:24.930
And we've been talking to Rush,

00:34:24.931 --> 00:34:28.760
which manages a lot of low
income clinics around Chicago.

00:34:28.761 --> 00:34:32.250
And then we're talking with
University of Arkansas,

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:34.390
their medical care system,

00:34:34.391 --> 00:34:36.760
which manages a large
rural population.

00:34:36.761 --> 00:34:40.310
So, we started doing some
initial design work with,

00:34:40.311 --> 00:34:41.760
you know?

00:34:41.761 --> 00:34:43.150
Yeah, so we're working with.

00:34:43.151 --> 00:34:45.756
It's kind of leading
up a lot of that.

00:34:45.757 --> 00:34:47.423
And so,
I'll just give you a flavor, so

00:34:47.424 --> 00:34:49.150
we started with
Northwestern Medicine.

00:34:49.151 --> 00:34:52.181
And we did a lot of interviews
with people in primary care

00:34:52.182 --> 00:34:55.223
clinics, and quickly realized
it was never gonna fly.

00:34:55.224 --> 00:34:56.587
There was no way that
we were gonna put

00:34:56.588 --> 00:34:57.830
this into a primary care clinic.

00:34:57.831 --> 00:34:59.500
Because there's no bandwidth,
nobody has time,

00:34:59.501 --> 00:35:01.420
they don't have the expertise.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:04.620
So, that's where
the patients are,

00:35:04.621 --> 00:35:07.820
but there's no staff
who can manage it.

00:35:07.821 --> 00:35:11.380
And what we realized is that
this organization, a lot

00:35:11.381 --> 00:35:13.930
of organizations are developing
these sort of centralized care

00:35:13.931 --> 00:35:16.010
systems in
Northwestern Medicine is.

00:35:16.011 --> 00:35:18.298
So it's a centralized
care management system,

00:35:18.299 --> 00:35:21.145
where they're getting,
when physicians make referrals,

00:35:21.146 --> 00:35:23.395
they're making sure that
patients get there.

00:35:23.396 --> 00:35:25.150
So they'll call them up,
they'll work with them.

00:35:25.151 --> 00:35:27.348
When they have problem patients,
they'll stay engaged with them

00:35:27.349 --> 00:35:29.044
to try to make sure they get
the care that they need.

00:35:29.045 --> 00:35:32.830
So these are people that are
delivering their care remotely.

00:35:32.831 --> 00:35:35.460
And they're often dealing
patients that are kind of

00:35:35.461 --> 00:35:37.850
difficult, or
that have problems.

00:35:37.851 --> 00:35:40.244
So, we realized that's
probably the place.

00:35:40.245 --> 00:35:43.997
So we've started, we've done
a first round of detailed

00:35:45.783 --> 00:35:50.200
First round of interviews
with these care managers.

00:35:50.201 --> 00:35:53.230
And we found a number of
barriers that have problems.

00:35:53.231 --> 00:35:58.504
So, they don't see their role
as managing mental health,

00:35:58.505 --> 00:35:59.882
necessarily.

00:35:59.883 --> 00:36:03.344
They manage medical problems and
lifestyle problems, obesity,

00:36:03.345 --> 00:36:03.910
and so on.

00:36:03.911 --> 00:36:07.190
But they haven't seen mental
health as part of what they do.

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:10.880
Honestly, they don't really
know what their role is,

00:36:10.881 --> 00:36:13.990
cuz they're developing it, and
it's kind of a chaotic system.

00:36:13.991 --> 00:36:17.693
But they also most of them have
no training in mental health.

00:36:17.694 --> 00:36:20.127
But on the plus side, more than
half of their patients when we

00:36:20.128 --> 00:36:21.340
go into the medical records.

00:36:21.341 --> 00:36:22.700
More than half of their patients

00:36:22.701 --> 00:36:24.470
have significant
levels of depression.

00:36:24.471 --> 00:36:28.240
So this is a population
where that's a huge problem.

00:36:28.241 --> 00:36:31.093
And they recognized that the
depression interferes with their

00:36:31.094 --> 00:36:32.660
ability to get
other things done.

00:36:32.661 --> 00:36:34.840
So if they have diabetes,
they know that the people that

00:36:34.841 --> 00:36:38.260
are depressed have a harder
time managing their diabetes.

00:36:38.261 --> 00:36:40.480
So they recognize that
there's a problem.

00:36:41.870 --> 00:36:45.970
There's a lot of barriers
to mental health referral.

00:36:45.971 --> 00:36:49.745
So, either long waits, or
just lack of availability.

00:36:49.746 --> 00:36:53.723
And they actually see
this type of system as

00:36:53.724 --> 00:36:56.273
potentially beneficial.

00:36:56.274 --> 00:36:58.926
So, rather than going into
a primary care where we have to

00:36:58.927 --> 00:37:01.801
find somebody and tell them to
do something in addition to what

00:37:01.802 --> 00:37:03.400
they're already doing.

00:37:03.401 --> 00:37:05.690
Here we could potentially
develop a system that can make

00:37:05.691 --> 00:37:06.620
their jobs easier.

00:37:06.621 --> 00:37:08.690
And if we can develop a system
that makes somebody's job

00:37:08.691 --> 00:37:12.620
easier, it is much more
likely to be implemented

00:37:12.621 --> 00:37:13.610
in a sustainable manner.

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:20.091
So, so far in this setting,
they're very enthusiastic,

00:37:20.092 --> 00:37:24.649
and we're starting to do
the same work with Rush and

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:29.114
with the University of
Arkansas Medical Center.

00:37:29.115 --> 00:37:34.990
So that's kind of, I think,
yeah, okay, so okay.

00:37:34.991 --> 00:37:38.382
So briefly, so I think what
we're looking at here is

00:37:38.383 --> 00:37:42.391
the primary care is still, the
doc is still the primary person

00:37:42.392 --> 00:37:44.644
that is connected
to the patient.

00:37:44.645 --> 00:37:47.528
Specialty mental health may or
may not be available, but

00:37:47.529 --> 00:37:49.942
we now increasingly have
these care managers.

00:37:49.943 --> 00:37:53.279
And if we can put something in
the center there that can manage

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:56.093
communications between
those that can monitor and

00:37:56.094 --> 00:37:59.380
help monitor the patient that
can provide support and can.

00:37:59.381 --> 00:38:02.210
We may be able to get something
would actually sustainable

00:38:02.211 --> 00:38:02.870
and workable.

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:05.863
I'm gonna skip through this.

00:38:05.864 --> 00:38:09.245
This is sort of the,
this is the summary, but

00:38:09.246 --> 00:38:13.881
in interest of time, I wanna get
to some of the sensing stuff.

00:38:13.882 --> 00:38:16.400
Cuz I wanted to talk with some
of the people here about that.

00:38:19.393 --> 00:38:23.943
And just briefly, you can think
about these digital mental

00:38:23.944 --> 00:38:28.403
health tools particularly
the skills based ones as little

00:38:28.404 --> 00:38:30.601
feedback devices, right?

00:38:30.602 --> 00:38:33.121
So they get information in,
they process it, and

00:38:33.122 --> 00:38:36.121
they provide that information
back to the user in some way,

00:38:36.122 --> 00:38:38.020
that's hopefully useful to them.

00:38:38.021 --> 00:38:40.770
Provide some insight,
some motivation, right?

00:38:40.771 --> 00:38:43.496
And the problem that a lot of
users encounter is putting

00:38:43.497 --> 00:38:44.984
the information in, right?

00:38:44.985 --> 00:38:45.783
We've all probably

00:38:45.784 --> 00:38:47.710
downloaded apps that we
think is gonna be great.

00:38:47.711 --> 00:38:49.248
And then we use them for
a few days and

00:38:49.249 --> 00:38:51.615
get sick in the information and
uninstall it, right?

00:38:51.616 --> 00:38:56.163
So that's where potentially
having some way of acquiring

00:38:56.164 --> 00:39:00.820
those data like in the same way
we can get step counters now.

00:39:00.821 --> 00:39:05.410
Have made a big impact on people
who wanna be physically active,

00:39:05.411 --> 00:39:06.410
it maybe helpful.

00:39:06.411 --> 00:39:11.197
So, just a little bit of
the work that we've done.

00:39:11.198 --> 00:39:14.645
When we started a few years ago,
we developed an app,

00:39:14.646 --> 00:39:15.650
Purple Robot.

00:39:15.651 --> 00:39:19.910
They can pull the sensor data
off of Android phones, and

00:39:19.911 --> 00:39:21.050
this is our first.

00:39:21.051 --> 00:39:24.930
Mostly, we did this to try
to debug the software, but

00:39:24.931 --> 00:39:28.400
we enrolled about 30 people, and

00:39:28.401 --> 00:39:31.280
there was a pretty good
range of depression.

00:39:31.281 --> 00:39:33.320
And so,
this is two weeks of data, and

00:39:33.321 --> 00:39:37.040
these are some clusters
from GPS that we got.

00:39:37.041 --> 00:39:40.580
So, I need some features
from GPS features.

00:39:40.581 --> 00:39:42.370
And so you see here,
location variance,

00:39:42.371 --> 00:39:45.220
the the variety of
places a person goes was

00:39:45.221 --> 00:39:48.130
pretty strongly related to
their level of depression.

00:39:48.131 --> 00:39:51.860
So, people who tend to go to the
same place all the time are more

00:39:51.861 --> 00:39:52.829
likely to be depressed.

00:39:53.990 --> 00:39:59.590
But novelty, people are less
likely to be depressed.

00:39:59.591 --> 00:40:02.549
Entropy, if you spend more time,

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:07.344
if your time is distributed
across places more evenly,

00:40:07.345 --> 00:40:10.623
you're less likely
to be depressed.

00:40:10.624 --> 00:40:13.521
If you tend to spend most of
your time at home or at work,

00:40:13.522 --> 00:40:15.222
like it says home-stay there,

00:40:15.223 --> 00:40:17.240
you're more likely
to be depressed.

00:40:17.241 --> 00:40:19.940
And the one that I was
particularly interested in here

00:40:19.941 --> 00:40:21.550
was circadian movement.

00:40:21.551 --> 00:40:25.695
So this is people who tend to be
more rhythmic in their movement

00:40:25.696 --> 00:40:27.740
through geographic space.

00:40:27.741 --> 00:40:31.120
They were less likely
to be depressed.

00:40:31.121 --> 00:40:34.763
So it seem a mood
would pull people off.

00:40:34.764 --> 00:40:40.190
So this was interesting, so we
thought, Could be just chance.

00:40:40.191 --> 00:40:43.224
So we got another dataset
from Andrew Campbell,

00:40:43.225 --> 00:40:46.110
who had collected
a student life dataset, and

00:40:46.111 --> 00:40:47.594
run the same analysis.

00:40:47.595 --> 00:40:48.825
So these were,

00:40:48.826 --> 00:40:54.133
about 50 students who they got
their PHQ9 measure of depression

00:40:54.134 --> 00:40:58.870
at the beginning of the semester
at the end of ten weeks.

00:40:58.871 --> 00:41:01.620
And then collected their GPS
data over this ten weeks.

00:41:01.621 --> 00:41:03.460
Now you see here
is the baseline,

00:41:03.461 --> 00:41:05.270
the number of places
that they went.

00:41:05.271 --> 00:41:08.660
Location variance and circadian
movement were significant.

00:41:08.661 --> 00:41:10.690
Not as high as in
the first sample, but

00:41:10.691 --> 00:41:11.940
they still remained significant.

00:41:11.941 --> 00:41:16.020
And at the end, there was
a pretty broad correlation.

00:41:16.021 --> 00:41:19.360
A lot of different
GPSHs related.

00:41:19.361 --> 00:41:22.690
So the general idea that
movement through space was

00:41:22.691 --> 00:41:26.240
related to depression
seems to be confirmed. Yeah.

00:41:26.241 --> 00:41:27.321
&gt;&gt; Sorry, what was this,

00:41:27.322 --> 00:41:30.740
what happened between the what
did you have the people?

00:41:30.741 --> 00:41:31.440
&gt;&gt; They went to school.

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:35.760
These were students at the
beginning of their quarter and

00:41:35.761 --> 00:41:36.680
at the end of the quarter.

00:41:36.681 --> 00:41:37.992
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; We have haven't do anything,

00:41:37.993 --> 00:41:39.496
or Andrew didn't have
them do anything.

00:41:39.497 --> 00:41:40.944
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:41:40.945 --> 00:41:42.112
&gt;&gt; Just a regular life.

00:41:42.113 --> 00:41:47.268
So we looked at, so one of
the things, I don't have a slide

00:41:47.269 --> 00:41:52.425
on this, one of the things that
I wondered if this is really

00:41:52.426 --> 00:41:58.640
true then probably we would see
stronger effect on weekends.

00:41:58.641 --> 00:42:00.263
Because during the week,

00:42:00.264 --> 00:42:03.293
their movement is less
under their own control.

00:42:03.294 --> 00:42:06.383
They have to go to class,
they have to do this or that.

00:42:06.384 --> 00:42:09.661
On the weekends, they're
free and indeed we saw for

00:42:09.662 --> 00:42:13.622
a number of these features they
were stronger on the weekends.

00:42:13.623 --> 00:42:17.173
Another thing we looked at was
If at the baseline depression,

00:42:17.174 --> 00:42:18.890
does that predict movement?

00:42:18.891 --> 00:42:21.468
So if you know how depressed I
am today, does that tell you

00:42:21.469 --> 00:42:24.213
anything about how I'm gonna
move through geographic space

00:42:24.214 --> 00:42:26.652
next week, five weeks from now,
ten weeks from now?

00:42:26.653 --> 00:42:28.612
You know, it's largely
what we would've expected.

00:42:28.613 --> 00:42:32.563
So, at the same measurements
taken at the same time there was

00:42:32.564 --> 00:42:34.810
sort of a modest correlation,
and

00:42:34.811 --> 00:42:37.613
that all went towards
zero as time went on.

00:42:37.614 --> 00:42:39.133
And then we look at
it the other way.

00:42:39.134 --> 00:42:44.454
So the GPS feature is predicting
depression 10 weeks later and

00:42:44.455 --> 00:42:47.212
what, we were surprised to see,

00:42:47.213 --> 00:42:51.662
I was actually was surprised
that there is no change.

00:42:51.663 --> 00:42:56.166
So, how people move through
geographic space tells you just

00:42:56.167 --> 00:42:59.255
as much about how
depressed I am today as it

00:42:59.256 --> 00:43:03.422
does about how depressed I'm
going to be in 10 weeks.

00:43:03.423 --> 00:43:06.215
And another slide I don't have,
I should have had,

00:43:06.216 --> 00:43:08.322
is the amount of
change in depression.

00:43:08.323 --> 00:43:10.135
And these people went up and
down, so

00:43:10.136 --> 00:43:12.790
there's a lot of
movement in this sample.

00:43:12.791 --> 00:43:14.194
And they're not,
they're students.

00:43:14.195 --> 00:43:18.283
They're not necessarily
all mentally doing fine.

00:43:18.284 --> 00:43:21.869
So, what this suggest here
is that how we move through

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:25.457
geographic space may be
an early warning detector for

00:43:25.458 --> 00:43:28.888
depression before people
start having symptoms,

00:43:28.889 --> 00:43:32.396
one of the things we might
see is social withdrawal or

00:43:32.397 --> 00:43:34.732
changes in patterns of movement.

00:43:34.733 --> 00:43:40.259
At the same time we published
our first article, fortunately

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:45.373
Micro Mucolesi was doing
the same thing at UCL in London.

00:43:45.374 --> 00:43:48.854
And found similar features,
we were fortunately, for

00:43:48.855 --> 00:43:52.113
one time in my life I actually
didn't get scooped and

00:43:52.114 --> 00:43:54.571
we got published
a month before he did.

00:43:54.572 --> 00:44:00.430
There's another group in Oxford,
that did the same kinda thing.

00:44:00.431 --> 00:44:02.955
These are actually using our
features and they did it with

00:44:02.956 --> 00:44:05.280
bipolar patients and
they can predict depression.

00:44:05.281 --> 00:44:09.043
So this is, yeah this is looking
good so we got our first study,

00:44:09.044 --> 00:44:12.243
then we replicated it,
now got independent people.

00:44:12.244 --> 00:44:14.484
So we did another study that we,

00:44:14.485 --> 00:44:18.385
where we got 211 people from
around the country, and

00:44:18.386 --> 00:44:22.053
different people we collected
this over the space.

00:44:22.054 --> 00:44:25.538
They were enrolled
in six weeks and

00:44:25.539 --> 00:44:29.983
we got their depression
at the beginning and

00:44:29.984 --> 00:44:34.909
the end and tracked a bunch
of different stuff and

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:38.173
it got nothing, it was really.

00:44:38.174 --> 00:44:41.573
So we thought maybe this
was done over the winter.

00:44:41.574 --> 00:44:46.171
Maybe it's going outdoors
in Minneapolis in

00:44:46.172 --> 00:44:49.803
December is different
than Miami.

00:44:49.804 --> 00:44:52.471
Maybe we had a broader range
of people older people move

00:44:52.472 --> 00:44:54.221
differently than younger people.

00:44:54.222 --> 00:44:58.840
We looked at a lot of the look
like younger people we've got

00:44:58.841 --> 00:44:59.791
an effect.

00:44:59.792 --> 00:45:02.792
But a lot of these we
just got no significance.

00:45:02.793 --> 00:45:09.081
And you know, it is,
was very disappointing but yeah?

00:45:09.082 --> 00:45:12.587
&gt;&gt; So, don't you have a baseline
or control for these people so

00:45:12.588 --> 00:45:15.476
that they can compare you
know whether people have

00:45:15.477 --> 00:45:18.872
been [INAUDIBLE] in the Winter,
compared to these people.

00:45:18.873 --> 00:45:21.188
So that you have some sort of
baseline as opposed to non

00:45:21.189 --> 00:45:23.716
oppressed people because you
know warm rather than cold.

00:45:23.717 --> 00:45:26.789
&gt;&gt; Well we did have, we didSo I
should say that this group was

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.594
roughly divided into quarters.

00:45:28.595 --> 00:45:31.689
So one quarter had high
depression and high anxiety,

00:45:31.690 --> 00:45:34.027
one had high depression and
no anxiety,

00:45:34.028 --> 00:45:36.434
one had high anxiety and
no depression and

00:45:36.435 --> 00:45:38.932
one quarter that when
a healthy controls.

00:45:38.933 --> 00:45:41.581
&gt;&gt; Okay so when you compare
it against the baseline of

00:45:41.582 --> 00:45:44.244
the healthy versus how much
more or less [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:44.245 --> 00:45:44.908
&gt;&gt; We just couldn't

00:45:44.909 --> 00:45:45.487
see anything.

00:45:45.488 --> 00:45:48.328
&gt;&gt; Couldn't see anything.

00:45:48.329 --> 00:45:53.524
&gt;&gt; I think, I don't think
&gt;&gt; Coasts versus the midwest?

00:45:53.525 --> 00:45:56.444
&gt;&gt; We did not, we looked at, I
mean there's probably ways that

00:45:56.445 --> 00:45:58.920
we can do this to try to
look at this more carefully.

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:02.273
There's a lot of issues in here.

00:46:02.274 --> 00:46:05.422
I don't necessarily believe
this is the final story but

00:46:05.423 --> 00:46:06.762
I think what this does,

00:46:06.763 --> 00:46:09.911
the message I think that I'm
getting this is that this is

00:46:09.912 --> 00:46:13.194
a much more complicated issue
than we thought initially.

00:46:13.195 --> 00:46:18.791
So, the other studies are small,
they tend to be geographically

00:46:18.792 --> 00:46:22.983
defined in a small area
a narrow scope of people.

00:46:22.984 --> 00:46:28.773
And I'll talk about this in a
moment, we'll talk about it now.

00:46:28.774 --> 00:46:32.099
I think what we're dealing
with is an enormous amount of

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:34.364
variability that
you have to manage,

00:46:34.365 --> 00:46:38.063
that you've got differences in
how people use their phones.

00:46:38.064 --> 00:46:42.578
In weather, in, you know,
health, in age, and, I mean just

00:46:42.579 --> 00:46:47.203
a huge amount of variability
that, you know, any small study.

00:46:47.204 --> 00:46:50.050
I mean, all these studies
are 10, or 30 people,

00:46:50.051 --> 00:46:53.593
and mostly done with students,
you know, the, and CS students,

00:46:53.594 --> 00:46:55.962
probably not general students,
you know.

00:46:55.963 --> 00:47:00.330
So the, when you move this out
and a lot of the studies also, I

00:47:00.331 --> 00:47:04.871
think that part of that is that
in CS, you, to get published you

00:47:04.872 --> 00:47:08.494
have to do something that
nobody's done before.

00:47:08.495 --> 00:47:14.082
And so it's not replicability,
that's not highly prized.

00:47:14.083 --> 00:47:15.260
But in public health,

00:47:15.261 --> 00:47:17.682
we have to have things
that you can replicate.

00:47:17.683 --> 00:47:21.673
And so, I think there's a lot
of room that where we can

00:47:21.674 --> 00:47:25.602
work together in this but
it's not gonna be simple.

00:47:25.603 --> 00:47:27.961
So I think we're
almost out of time, so

00:47:27.962 --> 00:47:31.608
I'm just gonna kind of wrap up
and what we're doing here is,

00:47:31.609 --> 00:47:35.112
what we're doing now is we just
got another grant funded to

00:47:35.113 --> 00:47:38.286
extend this and we're
started to extend this work.

00:47:38.287 --> 00:47:41.147
And we're partnering
also now with UCLA so

00:47:41.148 --> 00:47:44.802
UCLA has a large project
that they're starting called

00:47:44.803 --> 00:47:47.112
the Depression Grand Challenge.

00:47:47.113 --> 00:47:51.644
So they are, their goal is
to enroll 100,000 people and

00:47:51.645 --> 00:47:55.408
track them over 10 to 15
years with everything,

00:47:55.409 --> 00:47:57.973
genetics All kinds
of stuff cuz they

00:47:57.974 --> 00:48:02.092
wanna solve the problem of
depression that's the goal.

00:48:02.093 --> 00:48:04.992
And this is kind of the highest
level of the university

00:48:04.993 --> 00:48:06.802
that they made this initiative.

00:48:06.803 --> 00:48:10.692
And so we're working with them
to try to start doing this kind

00:48:10.693 --> 00:48:12.762
of sensing to get this sensing.

00:48:12.763 --> 00:48:16.847
So the next year or two, they're
planning on enrolling around

00:48:16.848 --> 00:48:20.856
10,000 people, and we're
trying to get this, get for

00:48:20.857 --> 00:48:23.556
at least a large segment
of those people,

00:48:23.557 --> 00:48:27.509
good software on their phones
where we can pull the data off.

00:48:27.510 --> 00:48:31.550
So that's kind of
the direction we're going in.

00:48:31.551 --> 00:48:33.347
And I think to solve
these kinds of problem,

00:48:33.348 --> 00:48:35.007
that's what we're
going to need to do.

00:48:35.008 --> 00:48:38.607
You can also say that what we
did get out of this are things

00:48:38.608 --> 00:48:40.531
like we could in this sample.

00:48:40.532 --> 00:48:44.305
We could see when people woke up
and when they went to bed within

00:48:44.306 --> 00:48:48.312
a ten minute window and we could
get that within 90% accuracy.

00:48:48.313 --> 00:48:50.730
We could get the relationship
to their contacts.

00:48:50.731 --> 00:48:55.937
Was it a Friend, a family
member, because how you text and

00:48:55.938 --> 00:49:00.627
phone is different in those
different categories.

00:49:00.628 --> 00:49:01.618
So, we can predict that.

00:49:01.619 --> 00:49:04.333
There are things that we can
predict more reliably and

00:49:04.334 --> 00:49:07.409
I think those are going to be
behaviors that are more proximal

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:08.568
to the phone sensors.

00:49:08.569 --> 00:49:13.319
If there's a direct line from
the sensor to the behavior, It

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:18.086
is probably going to be easier
or more reliably detectable.

00:49:18.087 --> 00:49:21.365
You get things like depression,
which is vague and

00:49:21.366 --> 00:49:24.265
expressed very
differently across people,

00:49:24.266 --> 00:49:27.864
it is going to be a lot harder
to get reliable detection.

00:49:27.865 --> 00:49:30.883
I'm going to end it here,

00:49:30.884 --> 00:49:35.825
just say thank you to
a lot of people cuz this

00:49:35.826 --> 00:49:40.920
wasn't just my work this
is a lot of people.

00:49:40.921 --> 00:49:42.232
So, thank you very much.

00:49:42.233 --> 00:49:47.544
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; It makes you feel better,

00:49:47.545 --> 00:49:51.170
In fact they found closeness
to home and closeness to work.

00:49:51.171 --> 00:49:54.635
That the two most highly
predictive features in machine

00:49:54.636 --> 00:49:55.747
learning model.

00:49:55.748 --> 00:49:57.199
For how to press [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:57.200 --> 00:49:59.947
And it went both ways,
if you loved work, then it was

00:49:59.948 --> 00:50:02.762
a super-good predictor of
you not being depressed.

00:50:02.763 --> 00:50:04.937
You hated your job [LAUGH], so

00:50:04.938 --> 00:50:08.572
we saw some location
based information, too.

00:50:08.573 --> 00:50:12.881
&gt;&gt; Yeah I think there's
something there, And

00:50:12.882 --> 00:50:17.174
it's interesting because I don't
think we've ever been able

00:50:17.175 --> 00:50:19.692
to really track it
that well before.

00:50:19.693 --> 00:50:25.075
So I sort of started looking at,
some geographers have used this.

00:50:25.076 --> 00:50:28.825
There's a guy in Israel,
I'm trying to remember his name.

00:50:28.826 --> 00:50:30.733
Noam, I can't think of it.

00:50:30.734 --> 00:50:33.912
Anyway, so
he's been funded by the various,

00:50:33.913 --> 00:50:38.151
the Israeli Tourist Board and
the Italian Tourist Board to use

00:50:38.152 --> 00:50:40.692
for sensing for
all kinds of things.

00:50:40.693 --> 00:50:42.145
But he's interested in mood.

00:50:42.146 --> 00:50:45.905
And so he had this great study
in Jerusalem where they got

00:50:45.906 --> 00:50:50.151
5,000 tourists and they were
collecting their GPS data.

00:50:50.152 --> 00:50:53.161
They, I think they got
some kind of GSR sensor,

00:50:53.162 --> 00:50:56.927
like a wrist-worn sensor and
then they got their mood, and

00:50:56.928 --> 00:50:59.490
he created the mood
map of Jerusalem.

00:50:59.491 --> 00:51:04.260
And it's like, you can see
elevations that are reflected

00:51:04.261 --> 00:51:09.222
across all of these as people
get closer to the Wailing Wall,

00:51:09.223 --> 00:51:14.089
and it's just really amazing
how like the geography of that

00:51:14.090 --> 00:51:16.933
city can impact people's moods.

00:51:16.934 --> 00:51:19.940
So I think there's a lot
that we can learn.

00:51:19.941 --> 00:51:21.914
People's rhythms
are reflected in it.

00:51:26.090 --> 00:51:31.002
Other questions or
comments or critiques?

00:51:31.003 --> 00:51:35.063
&gt;&gt; So when you say about
the connecting sensitive data

00:51:35.064 --> 00:51:39.417
on the phones, is that
specifically about location?

00:51:39.418 --> 00:51:42.010
But going forward,
do you have other data?

00:51:42.011 --> 00:51:44.203
&gt;&gt; We've collected-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:44.204 --> 00:51:45.549
&gt;&gt; What we collected in

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:48.166
that last study,
it wasn't just GPS.

00:51:48.167 --> 00:51:52.870
Certainly call is lot used on
Android, so we have call and

00:51:52.871 --> 00:51:53.850
text logs.

00:51:53.851 --> 00:51:58.407
Light, So like the wake,

00:51:58.408 --> 00:52:02.802
the sleep wake,
time was a big factor.

00:52:02.803 --> 00:52:05.968
You can get a lot just knowing
when people go to bed the first

00:52:05.969 --> 00:52:08.174
time cuz people
are fairly consistent.

00:52:08.175 --> 00:52:10.995
But like also sound,

00:52:10.996 --> 00:52:16.017
light was a factor,
use of the phone.

00:52:16.018 --> 00:52:16.894
So we had a bunch of them.

00:52:16.895 --> 00:52:20.994
We did not get accelerometry cuz
that just burns up the battery.

00:52:22.863 --> 00:52:26.433
And we're trying other little
things like maybe if we can turn

00:52:26.434 --> 00:52:27.669
all the sensors on for

00:52:27.670 --> 00:52:31.036
like 30 seconds periodically
during the day, that will

00:52:31.037 --> 00:52:34.071
give us a sort of a burst of
data that would be useful.

00:52:34.072 --> 00:52:35.035
I don't know, but

00:52:35.036 --> 00:52:37.930
those are the kinds of things
